    cards: [
        { f: 'Normal contraction pattern vs. uterine dystocia patterns', b: 'Normal: 3–5 effective contractions in 10 min with relaxation between, producing progressive dilation. Dystocia: too few/weak contractions, or tachysystole with short, weak contractions (coupling/tripling) that don’t change the cervix.', ref: '19.1' },
        { f: 'What puts a pregnant person at risk for labor dystocia?', b: 'Power: induction, epidural, chorioamnionitis, fatigue, overdistention, grand multiparity. Passenger: LGA, malposition. Passage: age < 16 or > 40, obesity, short stature, pelvic shape, nulliparity. Psyche: anxiety, no support. Position: supine.', ref: '19.1' },
        { f: 'How does fetal dystocia affect the second stage?', b: 'A malpositioned head (persistent OP/OT, brow, face) can’t flex and rotate, so descent stalls → prolonged pushing, fatigue, operative or cesarean birth.', ref: '19.1' },
        { f: 'Bishop score: 1 cm, anterior, 50% effaced, −2 station, medium consistency', b: 'Dilation 1 + position 2 + effacement 1 + station 1 + consistency 1 = 6 (borderline; > 6–8 is favorable).', ref: '19.2' },
        { f: 'Oxytocin infusion basics', b: 'Pump, milliunits/min. Start 0.5–2, increase 1–2 every 15–30 min to adequate contractions; onset and half-life 3–5 min. Stop for tachysystole, nonreassuring FHR, suspected abruption/rupture. Watch water intoxication.', ref: '19.2' },
        { f: 'Contraindications to induction', b: 'Placenta previa, vasa previa, malpresentation, cord prolapse, previous classical cesarean, active genital herpes, pelvic structural abnormality, invasive cervical cancer, < 39 weeks without indication.', ref: '19.2' },
        { f: 'Misoprostol vs. dinoprostone', b: 'Misoprostol (PGE1): PO/vaginal 25–50 mcg q4–6h, cheap, room temp, can’t be removed. Dinoprostone (PGE2): 10 mg insert up to 12 h, refrigerated, expensive, removable if tachysystole. Neither after uterine surgery (VBAC).', ref: '19.2' },
        { f: 'Amnioinfusion: indications and contraindications', b: 'Indications: cord compression/variable decels, oligohydramnios, dilute meconium. Contraindications: fetal distress requiring birth, active herpes, previa, abruption, malpresentation. Monitor uterine pressure (overdistention).', ref: '19.2' },
        { f: 'Characteristics and risk factors of preterm labor', b: 'Labor 20 to 36 6/7 weeks. Causes: cervical insufficiency, uterine anomalies, infection, anomalies, overdistention (multiples, polyhydramnios). Risks: prior preterm birth, threatened abortion, age extremes, smoking, drugs, periodontal disease, PPROM.', ref: '19.3' },
        { f: 'Meconium-stained fluid: risks and fetal complications', b: 'Risks: postterm, fetal distress/hypoxia, cord compression. Complication: meconium aspiration → pneumonia. Labor: amnioinfusion, NICU team at birth; suction only if not vigorous.', ref: '19.3' },
        { f: 'Risk factors for chorioamnionitis', b: 'Prolonged labor, prolonged ROM, multiple vaginal exams, internal monitors, meconium, bacterial vaginosis, GBS colonization, epidural.', ref: '19.3' },
        { f: 'How is preeclampsia associated with fetal hypoxia in labor?', b: 'Hypertension damages and ages the placenta (uteroplacental insufficiency) → less O2 transfer during contractions → late decelerations, minimal variability, distress.', ref: '19.3' },
        { f: 'Emergency vaginal birth steps', b: 'Call for help, warm blankets, gently assist birth, suction if needed, skin-to-skin, dry and keep warm (priority), clamp and cut cord, don’t pull the cord; oxytocin per protocol after placenta.', ref: '19.3' },
        { f: 'Breech types and cord prolapse risk', b: 'Frank (most common, lowest prolapse risk, largest dilation) · complete · footling/incomplete (highest prolapse risk). Head doesn’t mold → entrapment.', ref: '19.3' },
        { f: 'Newborn complications with an eating disorder in pregnancy', b: 'SGA, fetal growth restriction, microcephaly, intraventricular hemorrhage (and reduced fetal reserve in labor).', ref: '19.4' },
        { f: 'Labor with aortic stenosis or pulmonary hypertension', b: 'Passive second stage (no Valsalva pushing), forceps/vacuum assisted birth, lateral recumbent position, cardiac monitoring, strict I&O.', ref: '19.4' },
        { f: 'Indications for operative vaginal delivery', b: 'Maternal exhaustion, nonreassuring FHR needing urgent birth, prolonged second stage, after-coming head in breech (forceps). Requires full dilation, ruptured membranes, low head, no CPD, consent.', ref: '19.5' },
        { f: 'Forceps vs. vacuum fetal complications', b: 'Forceps: facial lacerations, facial nerve damage, cephalohematoma, skull fracture, intracranial hemorrhage.\nVacuum: scalp marks, cephalohematoma, subgaleal hematoma, retinal and intracranial hemorrhage.', ref: '19.5' },
        { f: 'Options after a previous cesarean', b: 'Trial of labor (TOLAC) aiming for VBAC if one prior low transverse incision and suitable facility, or elective repeat cesarean. Poor candidates: classical/T incision, prior rupture, > 1 cesarean, macrosomia, unknown scar, obesity.', ref: '19.6' },
        { f: 'How does preeclampsia increase cesarean risk?', b: 'Placental damage → fetal distress; maternal deterioration (thrombocytopenia, organ dysfunction, seizure, stroke) may require delivery before labor can progress; cure is delivery of the placenta.', ref: '19.6' },
        { f: 'Vasa previa vs. velamentous insertion', b: 'Velamentous: cord inserts into membranes, vessels branch before the placenta. Vasa previa: those unprotected fetal vessels cross the cervix → rupture at ROM → fetal exsanguination → cesarean before labor/ROM.', ref: '19.6' },
        { f: 'Complications of fetal distress', b: 'Fetal hypoxia → metabolic acidosis, low Apgars, meconium aspiration, hypoxic-ischemic brain injury, death; emergency operative or cesarean birth.', ref: '19.7' },
        { f: 'Risk factors for uterine rupture', b: 'Previous cesarean or uterine surgery (esp. classical), uterine trauma, polyhydramnios (overdistention), prolonged labor, oxytocin/prostaglandin overstimulation.', ref: '19.7' },
        { f: 'HELPERR (shoulder dystocia)', b: 'Help · Episiotomy · Legs (McRoberts) · Pressure (suprapubic) · Enter (rotational maneuvers) · Remove posterior arm · Roll to all fours. Never fundal pressure.', ref: '19.7' },
        { f: 'Signs of uterine rupture', b: 'Sudden loss of FHR tracing and contractions, severe abdominal pain (or sudden pain relief), late/prolonged decels, ± vaginal bleeding, maternal shock.', ref: '19.7' },
        { f: 'How can the nurse decrease labor exhaustion?', b: 'Push only at the contraction peak, let the fetus labor down, offer fluids/food (calories), allow rest periods, position changes, encouragement.', ref: '19.8' },
        { f: 'Complications of a succenturiate lobe', b: 'Its vessels running through the membranes may cross the cervix (vasa previa → fetal bleeding); the lobe can be retained after birth → postpartum hemorrhage or infection.', ref: '19.9' },
        { f: 'The 4 T’s of PPH', b: 'Tone (uterine atony, 70%), Trauma (lacerations, rupture, inversion), Tissue (retained placenta/clots), Thrombin (coagulopathy).', ref: '19.9' },
        { f: 'Uterine inversion: signs and care', b: 'Hemorrhage, shock (incl. vagal), pelvic pain, fundus not palpable, mass in the vagina. Call for help, fluids/blood, provider repositions the uterus, then uterotonics; monitor for shock.', ref: '19.9' },
        { f: 'Normal lochia vs. bleeding from an undetected laceration', b: 'Normal: dark red with small clots, uterus firm, decreasing. Laceration: bright red, brisk, continuous trickle despite a firm, contracted uterus.', ref: '19.10' },
        { f: 'How does uterine atony alter perfusion to the extremities?', b: 'Atony → hemorrhage → hypovolemia → compensatory peripheral vasoconstriction shunting blood to vital organs → weak pulses, slow capillary refill, cool pale clammy skin.', ref: '19.10' },
    ],
